{"id": "ea680ef9-a8ab-4232-948d-0674f14a3b89", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll run a readiness review of your FSx for ONTAP file system: configuration, capacity headroom, performance limits, data protection, and recent health. Then I'll rank what to fix first. First I'm locating the file system and pulling its details and metrics.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_RWb8SNH48YcmiTygn75t6I", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "fsx-ontap-readiness-review", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics", "searching-logs", "investigating-infrastructure-changes", "discovering-topology"], "prompt": "[DEEP RESEARCH MODE]
{
  "task": "Pre-event readiness review of the Amazon FSx for NetApp ONTAP file system(s) ahead of a heavy quarter-end batch run next week (week of 2026-11-16). Identify anything that could cause trouble during the run and produce a prioritized fix-first list.",
  "context": "User wants to know what to fix before a heavy batch workload (likely large sequential/metadata-heavy reads and writes, many clients) hits their FSx for ONTAP file system. This is a read-only review: do NOT make any mutating AWS calls. Current time is 2026-11-09T20:04:51Z. The agent space is in account 012345678901_1 / us-east-1, but find FSx for ONTAP file systems across all associated accounts and likely regions (start with us-east-1). If multiple file systems exist, review all and note which one is most likely the batch target (e.g. by tags/names).",
  "resources": ["All FSx for ONTAP file systems (FileSystemType=ONTAP) in associated accounts", "Their SVMs, volumes, backups, and associated ENIs/subnets/security groups/route tables", "CloudWatch namespace AWS/FSx metrics"],
  "timeframe": "Last 30 days for trend metrics (use 14-day peaks and daily max/p99 where useful); last 7 days for events/alerts",
  "criteria": "Check and report concrete values for: (1) File system status/lifecycle, any admin or lifecycle failure messages, pending/in-progress administrative actions (storage/throughput scaling, updates), and a scheduled weekly maintenance window falling in the batch week; (2) SSD storage capacity: used vs provisioned, growth trend, projected usage at end of next week, SSD capacity auto-grow/ storage-efficiency settings, and per-volume fullness (StorageCapacityUtilization / volume used %), volumes near full, snapshot reserve and snapshot space consumption, tiering policy and capacity pool usage, inode (file count) usage vs max per volume; (3) Throughput and IOPS: provisioned throughput capacity and provisioned SSD IOPS vs observed peak DataReadBytes/DataWriteBytes/ DiskIops/ NetworkThroughputUtilization/ DiskThroughputUtilization/ FileServerDiskThroughputBalance and CPUUtilization (per-HA-pair if multi-pair), noting whether peaks already approach limits and how much headroom a heavier batch would have; burst credit behavior for gen1 file systems; (4) Deployment type (Single-AZ vs Multi-AZ, gen1 vs gen2, HA pairs), preferred/standby subnet, and any recent failover events; (5) Data protection: automatic backup retention and window (check the backup/maintenance windows don't collide with the batch run), recent backup success, snapshot policies, SnapMirror/replication if visible; (6) Networking and access: SVM endpoints, security group rules for NFS/SMB/iSCSI/ONTAP management ports, route table configuration for Multi-AZ endpoint IP range, AD join/DNS health for SMB SVMs, and any single-client/ENA limits if discoverable; (7) Service quota proximity (volumes per file system, throughput, SSD size) relevant to growth; (8) CloudWatch alarms existing on the file system (and note missing alarms for capacity, throughput, IOPS, CPU); (9) Recent infrastructure changes (last 14 days) to the file system, volumes, SVMs or networking that could matter. Output: a short severity-ranked list (Critical / High / Medium / Low) with evidence (resource IDs, metric values, timestamps), what to change and which are time-sensitive given FSx scaling cooldowns (e.g. throughput/SSD increases take time and have a ~6-hour wait between SSD increases, so flag anything that should be done now). Also list what you could not check due to missing permissions or data."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review FSx for ONTAP file system readiness for quarter-end batch"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "f9b75fae-7003-4978-a342-17afdf8c70df", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I should check the other regions too — us-west-2, us-east-2, ap-southeast-2, and ap-southeast-1 — alongside the us-east-1 details. I also note this file system was only created on 2026-11-02, so there's just a week of metrics available: it's a MULTI_AZ_2 setup with one HA pair, 384 MBps throughput, 3072 IOPS, and 1 TiB capacity, with maintenance scheduled Sundays at 06:00 UTC.

Working through the maintenance window timing, the next occurrence on Sunday 2026-11-15 falls just before the batch processing week starts (week of 11-16, Monday–Sunday), but the following maintenance window on Sunday 2026-11-22 would land inside that batch week — worth flagging. Backups run daily at 05:00 UTC with only 1 day of retention.

"}], "parent_id": "98e64631-4349-48bd-9656-caed064a07ba"}

{"id": "5c4c8cf2-d3a2-40bc-9594-3da1f83601f3", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "No alarms show up, and the route table looks fine—routes point correctly to the preferred AZ endpoint ENI. But I only see one route table associated with the FSx subnets, so I need to figure out whether client subnets actually use this same table, since the VPC CIDR is broader and other route tables might exist for client subnets that aren't showing up in this result.

Now I'm shifting to pulling CloudWatch metrics—planning to query CPU, and likely throughput/IOPS, at the file system and node level over the past 7 days with an hourly period, summarizing max/min/last values per metric using JMESPath aggregation across the returned datapoints.

"}], "parent_id": "98e64631-4349-48bd-9656-caed064a07ba"}

{"id": "0c345383-b4c7-439f-bc48-ed2d83765fb9", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The datapoint counts line up with the time window, with creation at 16:32 and zero bytes read confirming the filesystem is idle with no client traffic. Since the SVM shows misconfigured AD join, SMB access would be broken, though NFS might still function depending on how the SVM is set up.

The disk throughput balance sits near 99-100%, consistent with gen2 MULTI_AZ burst-based IOPS behavior, so that's not an anomaly worth flagging. Now I'm moving on to the storage metrics.

"}], "parent_id": "98e64631-4349-48bd-9656-caed064a07ba"}

{"id": "1c7b92c6-99bb-4489-94f4-ff3ae7f998d2", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The root cause is clear: the SVM is misconfigured due to invalid AD credentials, with two failed retry attempts. Now I'm verifying some documentation details—gen2 IOPS limits, scaling cooldowns, SSD sizing—and checking whether Multi-AZ failover events show up anywhere, since they aren't directly visible via API; I'll look at the FileServer metrics instead to see if they reveal anything.

I'm skipping the client SG rule review as unnecessary, and instead working out the backup/maintenance window overlap. The maintenance window format "7:06:00" translates to Sunday at 06:00 UTC, which falls right at the end of the batch week (2026-11-22), with the prior Sunday (11-15) also landing right before it—confirming weekdays using 11-09 as the reference Monday. Now I want to double-check the gen2 limits documentation to make sure I have the numbers right.

"}], "parent_id": "98e64631-4349-48bd-9656-caed064a07ba"}

